[h1]🧟 Knox Survivors[/h1]

Knox Survivors adds human survivors to Project Zomboid with the goal of making them feel like other people surviving alongside you.

The original idea was inspired by survivor mods like Superb Survivors, but Knox Survivors and its survivor framework have been built from the ground up by me, .exe.

My main goal is to keep everything feeling as close to vanilla Zomboid as possible.

Survivors use normal clothing, cosmetics, weapons, items, containers, animations, and game systems wherever possible instead of feeling disconnected from the base game.

They can survive on their own, scavenge, equip themselves, manage basic needs, fight, meet other survivors, form groups, settle around bases, and die.

[h1]🚧 Current State[/h1]

Knox Survivors is still actively being developed.

The current rebuild includes persistent survivors, autonomous survival behavior, recruitment, companion orders, groups, combat, scavenging, base work, and survivor management.

Some systems are still being improved, especially pathfinding, firearms, competing orders, base jobs, and long-running saves.

[b]Current focus:[/b] Single-player
[b]Current target:[/b] Project Zomboid Build 42.20–42.21 (42.21 compatibility is being tested)
[b]Fresh saves are strongly recommended while the rebuild is changing.[/b]

[h1]▶️ How To Play[/h1]

[b]1.[/b] Subscribe to Knox Survivors and its required item, [b]KnoxBridge Runtime[/b]. Let Steam finish downloading both.

[b]2.[/b] The Bridge Workshop item is only a dependency marker; its installer is downloaded separately. On Windows, download and run [url=https://github.com/exe-create/KnoxBridge/releases/latest]KnoxBridgeSetup.exe[/url]. On Linux/macOS, download and extract the KnoxBridgeRuntime ZIP, close Steam, and run [b]sh scripts/setup-unix.sh[/b] (Python 3 required; this path is not live-verified yet). No separate Java installation is needed.

[b]3.[/b] Enable Knox Survivors in the Mods menu and on a new disposable save. Launch Project Zomboid normally through Steam.

[b]4.[/b] KnoxBridge opens its full-screen [b]Review Java Mods[/b] approval gate at the main menu before world entry. It lists enabled-mod JARs; new or changed files are blocked by default and stay visible. Select a compatible module to review its SHA-256, then choose [b]Allow[/b] or [b]Keep denied[/b]. Each decision applies only to that exact JAR SHA-256. Turn on [b]Remember next choice[/b] to save the next decision across launches; leave it off for a one-launch choice. If you change your mind before continuing, the deny button becomes [b]Undo pending choice[/b]. Quit and relaunch once only when a choice changes which Java modules load. The displayed author is unverified mod metadata. JARs without KnoxBridge compatibility remain visible but cannot be loaded. The matching alpha8 Bridge update still requires live Build 42 verification.

[b]Save safety:[/b] the runtime installer changes startup configuration, not save files. Knox save compatibility is not certified for important existing saves; use a disposable new save and back up saves you care about.

[b]Compatibility:[/b] KnoxBridge loads enabled PZ mods that declare its module descriptor and implement the KnoxBridge API. Java mods for another loader do not load automatically; their authors must port/support KnoxBridge. Java modules run with full game-user permissions and are not sandboxed.

[b]Important:[/b] Use only one Java instrumentation runtime for Project Zomboid at a time. The retired Knox Launcher/legacy agent is unsupported.

